KUBERNETES_DAYS = {
71:{"title":"Kubernetes Architecture","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Kubernetes is a set of cooperating control-plane and node components that continuously reconcile declared state into running workloads.","architecture":"kubectl/clients → kube-apiserver → etcd\n                         ↓\n                 scheduler/controllers\n                         ↓\n                worker node: kubelet → runtime → Pods","how":["The API server is the front door to the control plane. Clients submit and read Kubernetes API objects through it, while etcd persists API state.","The scheduler watches for Pods that need a node and selects a suitable placement. Controllers continuously compare desired and observed state and request changes when they differ.","On workers, kubelet ensures Pod specifications are realized through a supported container runtime. Service networking is implemented by the cluster's data-plane components; the published architecture introduced kube-proxy as the node rule manager."],"commands":"kubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes\nkubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'","verify":"Inspect cluster and node state, then create a disposable workload and follow the object from API creation to scheduling and a running Pod. Do not infer control-plane health from one working `kubectl get` alone.","gotcha":"Kubernetes is not one giant container manager. Each component owns a different part of the reconciliation path, so 'Kubernetes is broken' is not yet a diagnosis.","security":"The API server, etcd and kubelet are high-value boundaries. Strong authentication/authorization, protected etcd access and secured node APIs matter because compromise can become cluster-wide.","lesson":"Troubleshooting improves when you know which component owns the decision you are questioning.","evidence":"Published Day 71 infographic artifact recovered from the project archive."},
72:{"title":"Kubernetes Pods","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"A Pod is Kubernetes' smallest deployable compute object and groups one or more tightly coupled containers that share a network context and selected storage.","architecture":"Pod\n├─ app container\n├─ optional helper container\n├─ shared Pod IP / port space / localhost\n└─ explicitly mounted volumes","how":["Most Pods contain one primary application container, while helper containers can live in the same Pod when they genuinely need the same lifecycle and network context.","Containers in one Pod share the Pod network namespace, including the same Pod IP and port space. They can communicate through localhost rather than discovering one another through cluster networking.","Volumes are shared only when mounted into the containers that need them. A Pod therefore creates a close coupling boundary, not a general-purpose replacement for a multi-service application topology."],"commands":"kubectl run web --image=nginx:alpine\nkubectl get pod web -o wide\nkubectl describe pod web\nkubectl exec web -- hostname -i","verify":"Create a Pod, inspect its assigned node and Pod IP, then run a command inside it. For a multi-container lab, verify localhost communication and explicitly shared volumes.","gotcha":"A Pod is not Docker Compose wearing a Kubernetes badge. Separate application services normally deserve separate controllers/Pods so they can scale and fail independently.","security":"Pod-level privilege settings, service-account tokens, host mounts and shared namespaces determine how much a compromised container can reach. Co-location increases trust between containers in the same Pod.","lesson":"The Pod is the scheduling and lifecycle unit, so container grouping should reflect genuine lifecycle coupling.","evidence":"Published Day 72 infographic artifact."},
73:{"title":"Kubernetes ReplicaSets","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"ReplicaSets continuously maintain a requested number of matching Pods and recreate missing replicas from a Pod template.","architecture":"ReplicaSet desired replicas + selector → matching Pods\n2/3 matching → controller creates 1 new Pod","how":["A ReplicaSet has one core responsibility: keep a specified number of Pods matching its selector running.","The selector and Pod-template labels connect the controller to the workload. If desired state is three and only two matching Pods exist, the controller creates another Pod from the template.","Deleting one managed Pod is a useful demonstration of reconciliation: the controller notices actual state no longer equals desired state and replaces the missing replica."],"commands":"kubectl apply -f replicaset.yaml\nkubectl get rs\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl describe rs web\nkubectl delete pod POD_NAME\nkubectl get pods -l app=web -w","verify":"Apply a three-replica ReplicaSet, delete one managed Pod and watch the controller restore the count. Confirm the replacement is a new Pod rather than the deleted object returning from the dead, because Kubernetes has boundaries even if YAML occasionally looks supernatural.","gotcha":"Selector and template labels must agree. A mismatch can make a controller fail validation or manage a different set of Pods than the operator intended.","security":"Replica count is availability logic, not access control. Apply Pod security, service-account and network policies separately.","lesson":"Controllers are Kubernetes' core mental model: state is maintained by reconciliation, not by hoping individual processes remain alive forever.","evidence":"Published Day 73 infographic artifact."},
74:{"title":"Kubernetes Deployments","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Deployments sit above ReplicaSets to manage stateless application rollouts declaratively, including replacement strategy and revision history.","architecture":"Deployment → ReplicaSet revision(s) → Pods","how":["A Deployment declares replicas, a selector, a Pod template and an update strategy. Kubernetes creates and manages ReplicaSets underneath it to move the workload toward the requested revision.","When `spec.template` changes, a Deployment can create a new ReplicaSet, scale it up and scale the old one down according to the rollout strategy instead of replacing every Pod manually.","The project lab keeps the manifest in the Kubernetes repository so rollout behavior is evidence in source control rather than a console action nobody can reproduce."],"commands":"kubectl apply -f day-74-deployments/deployment.yaml\nkubectl get deployments\nkubectl get rs\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl rollout status deployment/web\nkubectl rollout history deployment/web","verify":"Apply the Deployment, change the container image in the Pod template and apply again. Watch the new ReplicaSet and Pods appear while the old ReplicaSet scales down, then inspect rollout status/history.","gotcha":"Changing fields outside `spec.template` does not necessarily create a new Pod-template revision. Know which part of the object drives rollout behavior.","security":"Deployments repeat the Pod security context and image configuration across replicas, so unsafe defaults become fleet-wide. Pin trusted images and keep service-account/privilege settings explicit.","lesson":"ReplicaSets are the replica mechanics; Deployments add the release-management layer most stateless applications actually need.","evidence":"Published Day 74 infographic and project post; lab referenced `Enthernetcode/k8s-labs`."},
75:{"title":"Kubernetes Services","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"A Service gives changing Pods a stable network endpoint and selects its backend endpoints through labels.","architecture":"client → Service (stable virtual endpoint) → EndpointSlices → matching Ready Pods","how":["Pods are ephemeral and their IP addresses can change as controllers replace them. A Service lets clients depend on a stable service identity while Kubernetes tracks the matching backend Pods.","The published example selected Pods with `app: web`, listened on Service port 80 and forwarded to container targetPort 8080 as a ClusterIP service.","Service selection is label-driven. The Service object can exist and look perfectly healthy even when its selector matches no Pods, which is why endpoint inspection belongs in troubleshooting."],"commands":"kubectl apply -f service.yaml\nkubectl get svc\nkubectl describe svc web-service\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web-service\nkubectl get pods -l app=web --show-labels","verify":"Confirm the Service selector matches the intended Pod labels, inspect EndpointSlices and make a request through the Service from inside the cluster.","gotcha":"A Service selector that matches zero Pods leaves a stable endpoint with nowhere useful to send traffic. Inspect endpoints instead of admiring the Service object.","security":"Choose Service type intentionally. ClusterIP keeps the endpoint cluster-internal by default; NodePort/LoadBalancer change exposure and deserve matching network policy/firewall review.","lesson":"Stable service identity is separated from replaceable workload identity.","evidence":"Published Day 75 infographic artifact."},
76:{"title":"Kubernetes Namespaces","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published","summary":"Namespaces scope namespaced Kubernetes objects so environments and teams can organize resources without making object names globally unique across the cluster.","architecture":"cluster → namespace dev / namespace prod → namespaced workloads/services/config","how":["The same resource name can exist in different namespaces because namespaced API objects are addressed within that scope.","Commands can target a namespace explicitly with `-n`, or the current kubectl context can set a default namespace for convenience.","Namespaces are organizational and policy scopes, not automatic security isolation. RBAC, NetworkPolicy, quotas and admission controls determine what isolation actually exists."],"commands":"kubectl create namespace dev\nkubectl create namespace prod\nkubectl run web --image=nginx:alpine -n dev\nkubectl run web --image=nginx:alpine -n prod\nkubectl get pods -n dev\nkubectl get pods -n prod\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=dev","verify":"Create identically named disposable objects in two namespaces and prove queries/actions stay scoped. Then inspect RBAC/network policy separately rather than assuming namespace boundaries block access.","gotcha":"Deleting a namespace deletes the namespaced resources inside it. A tidy scope can become a very efficient deletion boundary.","security":"Use namespace-scoped RBAC, resource quotas and network policies where appropriate; do not market namespaces alone as tenant isolation.","lesson":"Namespacing organizes the API. Security comes from the controls applied to that scope.","evidence":"Published Day 76 journey content."},
77:{"title":"Kubernetes ConfigMaps","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"ConfigMaps separate non-sensitive application configuration from container images and can deliver values as environment variables or projected files.","architecture":"ConfigMap → env/envFrom OR projected volume → Pod/application","how":["Configuration such as environment names, URLs and logging levels can live in a ConfigMap instead of being baked into an image. The Pod then references the ConfigMap at runtime.","Environment-variable consumption is fixed for the lifetime of the running process. Updating the ConfigMap does not rewrite environment variables inside an existing container, so the workload must be restarted or replaced to consume new values.","Normal projected ConfigMap volume files can update eventually. Files mounted with `subPath` do not receive those automatic updates, which is the field detail that turns many apparently-correct configuration changes into stale containers."],"commands":"kubectl create configmap web-config --from-literal=APP_ENV=development --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=info -n dev\nkubectl get configmap web-config -n dev\nkubectl describe configmap web-config -n dev","verify":"Consume the same key once as an environment variable and once through a normal ConfigMap volume in a lab, update the ConfigMap and observe the different behavior. Repeat with `subPath` to prove it stays stale.","gotcha":"ConfigMap `subPath` mounts do not receive automatic updates. Normal projected volumes can update eventually; environment variables require a restart/replacement to change.","security":"ConfigMaps are for non-confidential values. Do not put passwords, tokens or private keys there merely because the object lives inside Kubernetes.","lesson":"Externalizing configuration only helps when you understand how and when the application actually receives updates.","evidence":"Published Day 77 infographic artifact; `subPath` behavior was the explicit field-knowledge anchor."},
78:{"title":"Kubernetes Secrets","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Kubernetes Secrets provide a dedicated object for sensitive configuration, but confidentiality still depends on access control, storage protection and application behavior.","architecture":"Secret → env / projected volume → Pod\nSecret object → etcd storage + RBAC/encryption controls","how":["A Secret keeps sensitive values out of the Pod specification and container image. It can be created imperatively or declared with fields such as `stringData`, which Kubernetes converts into the encoded `data` representation.","Base64 is encoding, not encryption. Anyone with read access to the Secret can decode the value. Real credentials therefore do not become safe to commit merely because Kubernetes stores a base64 representation.","Secret-backed environment variables are read when the container starts. Mounted Secret volumes can receive updates eventually, while `subPath` mounts do not receive automatic updates."],"commands":"kubectl create secret generic app-secret --from-literal=DB_USER=appuser --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=change-me\nkubectl get secret app-secret\nkubectl describe secret app-secret\nkubectl get secret app-secret -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d","verify":"Create only lab values, consume one through an environment variable and one through a volume, update the Secret and observe update behavior. Confirm an identity without Secret read permission cannot retrieve the object.","gotcha":"Base64 ≠ encryption. Also, Secret env vars do not auto-update and `subPath` volume mounts do not receive projected updates.","security":"Enable etcd encryption at rest where appropriate, apply least-privilege RBAC, rotate credentials, prevent apps from logging Secret contents and use external secret managers for higher-sensitivity environments when justified.","lesson":"Kubernetes gives secrets a dedicated delivery mechanism; it does not take responsibility for every layer of secret security.","evidence":"Published Day 78 infographic artifact and lab reference `k8s-labs/tree/main/day-78-secrets`."},
79:{"title":"Kubernetes Persistent Storage","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"PersistentVolumes, PersistentVolumeClaims and StorageClasses separate application data from the lifecycle of individual Pods.","architecture":"Pod → PVC → PV → storage backend\n          ↑\n     StorageClass / provisioner","how":["A PersistentVolume is cluster storage whose lifecycle is independent of an individual Pod. A PersistentVolumeClaim is a workload's request for storage, and a StorageClass can define how compatible storage is provisioned dynamically.","A claim typically declares an access mode, storage request and optionally a `storageClassName`. With a suitable class, the claim can trigger dynamic provisioning; otherwise a compatible PV must exist for binding.","The Pod references the PVC by name and mounts that volume into the container. The controller can replace the Pod while the claim remains connected to persistent data."],"commands":"kubectl get storageclass\nkubectl get pvc\nkubectl get pv\nkubectl describe pvc app-data\nkubectl get pv -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,RECLAIM:.spec.persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy","verify":"Create a PVC and Pod, write data, replace the Pod and prove the new Pod sees the same data. Inspect PVC events and binding state rather than waiting at `Pending` with spiritual optimism.","gotcha":"`ReadWriteOnce` means read-write mounting by a single node, not necessarily a single Pod. `ReadWriteOncePod` is the access mode intended for true single-Pod access when the driver supports it.","security":"Review the StorageClass, reclaim policy and backend encryption/access controls. Persistent storage survives workload replacement, so sensitive data can outlive the process that created it.","lesson":"Compute can be disposable without forcing application state to share the same lifecycle.","evidence":"Published Day 79 infographic artifact and `k8s-labs` manifests."},
80:{"title":"Kubernetes Ingress","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Ingress describes HTTP/HTTPS routing rules toward Services; an Ingress controller is the component that actually implements those rules.","architecture":"external client → Ingress controller → Service → Ready Pods","how":["A Service provides a stable endpoint for Pods, while Ingress adds HTTP-aware routing such as host and path rules in front of Services.","The Ingress resource is configuration. A controller watches those resources and configures a proxy/load balancer to implement them. Creating an Ingress object without a compatible controller does not expose the application.","IngressClass and controller selection matter in clusters with multiple controllers or explicit class requirements. TLS configuration also introduces certificate/secret ownership and termination decisions."],"commands":"kubectl get ingressclass\nkubectl get ingress\nkubectl describe ingress web\nkubectl get svc\nkubectl get endpointslices","verify":"Resolve the hostname to the intended ingress endpoint, send an HTTP/HTTPS request and trace the path from controller to Service to EndpointSlices/Pods. Confirm the controller actually observed the resource.","gotcha":"An Ingress resource with no controller is basically a very confident routing wish.","security":"Terminate TLS deliberately, protect certificate Secrets, restrict controller privileges and use NetworkPolicy/backend controls instead of assuming the ingress boundary alone secures workloads.","lesson":"Kubernetes separates the desired routing rule from the component that implements it, and debugging must keep those two objects distinct.","evidence":"Published Day 80 infographic artifact."},
81:{"title":"Helm","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Helm packages Kubernetes templates, defaults and metadata into configurable releases so environment differences do not require copy-paste manifests.","architecture":"Chart + values → template rendering → Kubernetes manifests → Helm release","how":["A chart normally includes `Chart.yaml` metadata, `values.yaml` defaults and a `templates/` directory containing Kubernetes resource templates.","Values let one chart render different environment configuration while preserving one package structure. Rendering can be inspected before cluster mutation, which is useful because template abstraction should not hide the resulting Kubernetes YAML.","Helm also tracks release state and upgrades. That operational release model is what makes it more than a fancy text substitution engine."],"commands":"helm lint ./myapp\nhelm template myapp ./myapp\nhelm upgrade --install myapp ./myapp --namespace prod --create-namespace\nhelm list -n prod\nhelm history myapp -n prod","verify":"Lint and render the chart, inspect the generated manifests, then install/upgrade in a lab and compare the release resources against the rendered intent.","gotcha":"A syntactically successful template render can still create semantically wrong Kubernetes objects. Inspect the rendered YAML and validate runtime behavior.","security":"Do not embed real credentials in chart values committed to source; review chart dependencies and generated RBAC/network exposure like any other infrastructure dependency.","lesson":"Copying YAML per environment works until configuration drift arrives with a chair.","evidence":"Published Day 81 infographic artifact."},
82:{"title":"Kubernetes Production Deployment","phase":"Kubernetes","status":"published-artifact","summary":"The Kubernetes capstone connected scope, configuration, secrets, controllers, probes, networking, storage, ingress and Helm into one production-style deployment flow.","architecture":"Client → Ingress → Service → Ready Pods\n                          ↙    ↓    ↘\n                  ConfigMap  Secret  Persistent Storage\nHelm → configurable release of the resource set","how":["Each resource has a separate responsibility: Namespace scopes namespaced objects, ConfigMap carries non-sensitive configuration, Secret carries sensitive configuration, Deployment manages replicas/rollouts, Service provides stable routing, PVC requests storage and Ingress describes external HTTP/HTTPS routing.","Readiness decides whether a Pod should receive normal Service traffic; liveness addresses whether a container should be restarted. Treating those probes as synonyms can turn a transient dependency issue into an avoidable restart loop.","Helm packages the objects into a release, but release status is not end-to-end verification. The real client path must still be tested through Ingress, Service, endpoints and Ready Pods."],"commands":"helm upgrade --install web ./web-chart --namespace prod --create-namespace --wait --timeout 5m\nkubectl get pods -n prod\nkubectl get svc -n prod\nkubectl get ingress -n prod\nkubectl get endpointslices -n prod","verify":"Deploy the release, inspect resource readiness, then issue a real client request through the ingress path. When traffic fails, validate Ingress → Service → EndpointSlices → Ready Pods rather than stopping at Helm status.","gotcha":"`Running` does not mean `receiving traffic`. A Pod can be running while readiness fails, a Service selector can match no usable endpoints, and `helm --wait` still does not test the real client request path.","security":"Review RBAC, Secret storage/encryption, Pod privilege, ingress exposure, TLS, NetworkPolicy and persistent-data controls as one connected system.","lesson":"A green Helm release is not an application-level health certificate; sadly, software refuses to respect our optimism.","evidence":"Published Day 82 infographic/post artifact; Kubernetes phase capstone."}
}
